<h2>Indian maize for export</h2>
<p>India has become a meaningfully larger maize exporter in recent years, driven by record domestic production — the 2025-26 crop is estimated at over 55 million tonnes, an unprecedented jump on the year before. Most of that exportable surplus moves regionally: Nepal takes roughly two-thirds of India's maize export volume by value, with Sri Lanka and Bhutan the next largest buyers, followed by smaller volumes into Bangladesh and a handful of further markets.</p>

<p>This is a bulk feed-grade trade rather than a specialty-corn business, and logistics reflect that — a meaningful share of Nepal-bound maize moves overland by road or rail rather than by container vessel, given the shared land border.</p>

<h2>Important: maize export volumes are shaped by domestic policy, not fixed</h2>
<p>Maize is currently exportable, and export volumes have grown substantially — first-half 2025-26 shipments ran roughly three times the prior year's pace. But two domestic factors are worth understanding if you're planning a recurring maize programme. First, the Indian government's ethanol-blending target pulls a meaningful share of domestic corn toward distilleries rather than export, so the exportable surplus available in a given season depends on how that competing demand plays out. Second, like several other Indian agri-commodities, maize export terms have been adjusted by government policy before in response to domestic price and supply conditions.</p>
<p><strong>What this means practically:</strong> we confirm current export terms and any applicable conditions at the time your order is placed, rather than quoting a fixed position on policy indefinitely into the future. If you're building a recurring maize supply programme, particularly at scale, it's worth discussing your timeline with us directly so we can flag any policy or supply-availability considerations early.</p>

<h2>How should I specify maize?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Moisture.</strong> 14% or below is standard for stable storage and transit — higher moisture risks mould and aflatoxin development, particularly on longer regional or sea routes.</li>
  <li><strong>Foreign matter and damaged kernels.</strong> Specify your tolerance explicitly — feed-grade contracts commonly run 2% foreign matter and 3-4% damaged/discoloured kernels, but buyers with tighter feed-mill specifications should state their own limits.</li>
  <li><strong>Delivery mode.</strong> Bulk (vessel or rail) suits large regional orders, especially into Nepal; bagged container loading suits smaller or more distant orders. Tell us your preference and destination and we'll quote the more efficient route.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Maize pricing</h2>
<p>Maize is a bulk commodity and prices move with the domestic Indian harvest, ethanol-sector offtake, and regional demand. Send us your quantity, destination, and delivery mode (bulk or containerised) and we'll quote current terms with a short validity window.</p>

